  "reason": "Evidence: RNA (pca_n2000, singleprofile) achieves 48.5% acc while image (resnet50, singleprofile) is only 20.8%, confirming RNA is the dominant signal. We have only one RNA data point, so uncertainty is high about which design choices matter most. Question tested: Does moving from singleprofile to multiprofile (with simple avgpool aggregation) improve RNA performance, holding the encoder constant at pca_n2000? This is a minimal, clean extension of config_001 that isolates the profile-setting effect. Why best next experiment: If multiprofile+avgpool helps, it validates a direction that unlocks many downstream configurations (transformer aggregation, better encoders, multimodal fusion) and justifies further investment there. If it does not help, we learn that singleprofile is sufficient and can redirect budget toward encoder improvements (scvi, scgpt) or multimodal fusion. Either outcome significantly reduces uncertainty about a core architectural axis before committing budget to more complex multimodal configurations."
}

